  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter Philosophy, Empathy & Craftsmanship. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 20: Katrina Owen from UGtastic Archive (2013).",
  "Katrina's talks and Exercism platform swept through Chicago user groups, establishing daily coding katas and empathetic code review as standard practice.",
  "What inspired Katrina Owen to build Exercism.io? How does thoughtful, asynchronous mentorship transform beginners into confident craftsmen? What principles guided Katrina Owen when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: qXfDi_BjtMk). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "Mentorship is not just pointing out errors; it is asking guiding questions. Effective AI collaboration requires the same Socratic review mindset. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 21. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
